    pr_ref:  # 5f70
    description: "- **Subject:** Relationship edges (extends / instantiates) resolve only to first-party targets; external bases and external constructors are dropped or demoted.\n- **Mechanic:** The relationship-edge producer emits a typed edge only when the target resolves to a first-party class symbol. For `extends`, an external/stdlib base yields `base_sym is None` and the loop `continue`s — no edge at all. For `instantiates`, an external constructor falls through to the generic call path and becomes a plain `calls` edge to an external_symbol placeholder. The whole relationship family lacks the external_symbol placeholder fallback that calls/imports/decorated_by already have.\n- **Expectation:** Every base-class and constructor relationship should be represented, with external targets routed to an external_symbol placeholder; the spec defines `extends` as \"class extends base class\" and `instantiates` as \"class instantiation (constructor call)\" with no first-party qualifier.\n- **Scope:** 49 of 209 Python classes with declared bases (23.4%) emit zero `extends` edges because all their bases are external (Exception×15, ModalScreen×12, Enum×6, App×3, Protocol×3, ABC, str, NamedTuple, …). `instantiates` records 0 external constructions out of 7939 edges; at least 2084 external constructor calls (Path×838, MagicMock×420, Console, etc.) are misfiled as plain `calls`.\n- **Exposure:** Any consumer doing type-hierarchy, reverse-slice, \"what is this a subclass of,\" or external-type-usage analysis; an LLM agent asked to find all Exception subclasses, all Enum classes, or all external types constructed gets the wrong answer.\n- **Consequence:** The map asserts by omission that error classes, enums, ABCs, and widgets have no base, and that external types are never constructed — substantively wrong structural facts that corrupt coupling and inheritance analysis.\n\n---\n\n(Filed from dogfooding.)"  # 5f70
    fields: {}  # 5f70
